# Stubbed symbols that ADI is known to call, and that are known to be harmless.
#
# These are the difference between an alarm worth reading and one people learn to ignore.
# Anything in this file logs at INFO and is not counted as a reason to distrust the session;
# anything NOT in this file logs at ERROR, because it means Apple's implementation started
# depending on something we only pretend to provide.
#
# Add to this list only with evidence - a passing end-to-end provisioning run with the call
# observed - never because a log line was annoying.

# Nothing is listed here now.
#
# makeWorkQueue used to be: called from a static constructor while dlopen'ing
# libstoreservicescore.so, and believed harmless because "our stub returns NULL". It did not. It
# returns a std::shared_ptr by value, through a buffer the caller provides, and the generated stub
# never wrote that buffer - so the constructor read a leftover stack value as a control block and
# incremented through it. That was issue #232. It is written by hand in
# libmediaplatform_handwritten.cpp now, and the lesson for this file is that "provisioning still
# worked" established nothing about what a stub leaves behind for its caller.
